-- title:
--   Typed primitives mod [q] for a special formal mathcal O_D-module
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, let $j_0\colon \mathbb Z_{q^2}=W(\mathbb F_{q^2})\to k$ be a ring homomorphism, and let $X_0$ be a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $k$ of type $j_0$: a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F=X_0.F$ together with a $\mathbb Z_{q^2}$-action $a\mapsto[a]$ and an endomorphism $\Pi$ with $\Pi\circ\Pi=[q]$ and $\Pi\circ[a]=[\sigma a]\circ\Pi$ ($\sigma$ the Witt-vector Frobenius), with the speciality and height-$4$ conditions. Write $I\subseteq k[[X_1,X_2]]$ for the ideal generated by the two components of the $q$-fold $F$-sum series $[q]_F$, and $I_2\subseteq k[[X,Y]]$ for the ideal generated by the components of $[q]_F(X)$ and of $[q]_F(Y)$; call $g$ with $g(0)=0$ primitive mod $q$ if $\partial_F g=g(F(X,Y))-g(X)-g(Y)\in I_2$. Assume the hypothesis `hdim`: there are primitive $e,e'$ mod $q$ which are linearly independent modulo $I$ and such that every primitive $g$ mod $q$ is congruent modulo $I$ to a $k$-linear combination of $e,e'$. Then there exists $g\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to k[[X_1,X_2]]$ such that each $g_i$ has zero constant term and is primitive mod $q$; the $g_i$ are linearly independent modulo $I$ (any $c$ with $\sum_i c_i g_i\in I$ vanishes); for every $a\in\mathbb Z_{q^2}$ one has $g_0\circ[a]\equiv j_0(a)\,g_0$ and $g_1\circ[a]\equiv j_0(\sigma a)\,g_1 \pmod I$; and for all $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb Z_{q^2}$, putting $s$ for the $F$-sum of $[\alpha]$ and $[\beta]\circ\Pi$, there are $c_0,c_1\in k$ with $g_0\circ s\equiv j_0(\alpha)\,g_0+c_0\,g_1$ and $g_1\circ s\equiv j_0(\sigma\alpha)\,g_1+c_1\,g_0 \pmod I$. Spanning of the space of primitives by $g_0,g_1$ is not asserted in the conclusion, although it is assumed of $e,e'$ in `hdim`.
--
--   This provides a typed basis, modulo the ideal generated by the $[q]$-series, of the primitives mod $q$ of a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module, diagonalising the $\mathbb Z_{q^2}$-action into the types $j_0$ and $j_0\circ\sigma$ and recording the off-diagonal shape of the action of elements $\alpha+\beta\Pi$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the argument to compute the trace of such an element on the space of primitives, in [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.trace_restrict_primitives_eq_intCast_of_charP_of_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.trace_restrict_primitives_eq_intCast_of_charP_of_dvd).
